        <body>
    <h2>Student Rights & Discipline in District 75: A Parent's Guide to Manifestation Determinations and Due Process</h2>

    <p>In District 75, which serves students with significant disabilities across New York City, understanding your child's rights regarding discipline is paramount. While all NYC students are entitled to fair treatment, students with Individualized Education Programs (IEPs) have additional safeguards under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Education Law. This guide focuses on Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), suspensions, and your due process rights within the District 75 context.</p>

    <h3>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): A Critical Safeguard</h3>
    <p>A Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is a critical process designed to protect students with IEPs from being disciplined for behavior that is a direct result of their disability or the school's failure to properly implement their IEP. According to IDEA (20 U.S.C. § 1415(k)(1)(E)) and New York State Part 200 Regulations (200.2(b)(1)(vii)), an MDR <b>must</b> be conducted whenever a student with an IEP faces:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>A suspension exceeding 10 consecutive school days.</li>
        <li>A series of suspensions that constitute a "pattern of removals" totaling more than 10 school days in a school year, if the behavior is substantially similar to the student's previous infractions, additional factors such as the length of each removal, the total amount of time the student has been removed, and the proximity of the removals to one another.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>What Happens During an MDR?</b></p>
    <p>The MDR is not just a formality. It's a thorough review conducted by the IEP team, including you, the parents/guardians, and relevant school personnel (teachers, special education providers, school psychologist, etc.).  The team must review all relevant information in the student's file, including:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>The student's IEP</li>
            <li>Teacher observations</li>
            <li>Any relevant information provided by the parents</li>
            <li>Evaluation results</li>
            <li>Any other documentation related to the student’s disability and behavior</li>
        </ul>

    <p>The IEP team then determines if the behavior in question was:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Caused by, or had a direct and substantial relationship to, the child's disability; or</b></li>
        <li><b>The direct result of the school district's failure to implement the IEP.</b></li>
    </ol>

    <p><b>Key Considerations During the MDR:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>IEP Implementation:</b>  Was the IEP being followed *exactly* as written?  This includes accommodations, modifications, related services, and behavioral supports.  Document any instances where the IEP was not implemented. This is often the most contentious point. Schools may claim they were implementing the IEP, while parents argue otherwise. Gather evidence (emails, teacher notes, lack of progress data) to support your claim.</li>
        <li><b>Behavioral Intervention Plan (BIP):</b> If the student has a BIP, was it up-to-date and consistently implemented? A BIP must be individualized to the student's specific needs and address the function of the challenging behavior.  If the BIP is ineffective, it needs to be revised. Under NYS regulations, a BIP is a REQUIRED element of an IEP when a student's behavior impedes their learning or the learning of others.</li>
        <li><b>Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA):</b> Has an FBA been conducted to determine the function of the student's behavior? An FBA is a critical tool for understanding *why* a student is engaging in challenging behavior.  If an FBA hasn't been conducted, it should be a top priority.  NYS Part 200 regulations REQUIRE an FBA and BIP when a student's behavior impacts learning.</li>
        <li><b>Communication:</b>  Was there effective communication between home and school regarding the student's behavior?  Lack of communication can be a red flag that the school is not adequately addressing the student's needs. Document all communication attempts and responses (or lack thereof).</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>What Happens if the Behavior *Is* a Manifestation?</b></p>
    <p>If the IEP team determines that the behavior *is* a manifestation of the student's disability or a result of IEP implementation failures, the following must occur (20 U.S.C. § 1415(k)(1)(F)):</p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>The IEP team must conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted.</b></li>
        <li><b>The IEP team must implement a Behavioral Intervention Plan (BIP) for the student; OR, if a BIP already exists, review and modify it, as necessary, to address the behavior.</b></li>
        <li><b>The student must be returned to their placement *before* the disciplinary action, unless the parents and the school district agree to a change of placement as part of the modification of the behavioral intervention plan.</b></li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>What Happens if the Behavior is *Not* a Manifestation?</b></p>
    <p>If the IEP team determines that the behavior is *not* a manifestation of the student's disability, the student can be disciplined in the same manner as students without disabilities. However, the school district must still provide Free and Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) to the student. This often means providing services in an alternative educational setting.</p>

    <h3>Advice for Suspensions in District 75: A Step-by-Step Guide</h3>
    <p>If your District 75 student is facing suspension, it's crucial to act quickly and strategically. Here's a step-by-step guide:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Immediately Request Documentation:</b>  Obtain a copy of the suspension notice, incident reports, witness statements, and any other documentation related to the suspension. Do this in writing (email is fine) and keep a copy for your records.  Knowing the specific allegations is essential.</li>
        <li><b>Notify the IEP Team:</b> Contact your child's teacher, special education providers, school psychologist, and any other relevant members of the IEP team *immediately*.  Document the date and time of your communication, and who you spoke with. Send an email summarizing the conversation to create a written record.</li>
        <li><b>Invoke Your Right to an MDR:</b> If the suspension is for more than 10 school days (consecutive or cumulative), *formally request* an MDR in writing.  Even if the school doesn't initiate it, your written request triggers the process.  State clearly that you believe the behavior is related to your child's disability or the IEP implementation.</li>
        <li><b>Prepare for the MDR Meeting:</b>  Gather all relevant information, including IEP documents, behavior plans, medical records, and any communication with the school.  Write down your concerns and be prepared to articulate *specifically* how the behavior is related to your child's disability or how the IEP was not implemented correctly.</li>
        <li><b>During the MDR Meeting:</b>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Bring an Advocate:</b> Consider bringing a special education advocate or attorney to the MDR meeting. They can help you understand your rights and advocate effectively for your child.</li>
                <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Take detailed notes during the meeting, including who said what.</li>
                <li><b>Focus on the IEP:</b>  Constantly refer back to the IEP and how it relates to the behavior.  Ask specific questions about IEP implementation.</li>
                <li><b>Don't Be Intimidated:</b>  Remember that you are an equal member of the IEP team.  Don't be afraid to ask questions and voice your concerns.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><b>After the MDR Meeting:</b>  Follow up with the school in writing, summarizing the outcome of the meeting and any agreed-upon actions.  If you disagree with the MDR determination, you have the right to file a complaint or request mediation or a due process hearing.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Common District 75 Pitfalls & Parent Advocacy Tips</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Lack of Individualization:</b> District 75 serves a diverse population of students. Ensure your child's IEP is truly individualized to their specific needs and not a generic template.</li>
        <li><b>Communication Barriers:</b>  Establish clear communication channels with the school.  Document all communication in writing. Request regular progress reports that are more detailed than the standard report card.</li>
        <li><b>Behavioral Expertise:</b> Advocate for staff with specialized training in behavior management and positive behavior interventions, especially those with experience working with students with similar disabilities.</li>
        <li><b>Placement Issues:</b>  Ensure that the placement is appropriate for your child's needs.  Visit the proposed placement and observe the classroom environment.</li>
        <li><b>Related Services:</b> Ensure that your child is receiving all mandated related services (speech therapy, occupational therapy, counseling, etc.) at the frequency and duration specified in the IEP.  Track attendance and service delivery.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Due Process Rights: Your Ultimate Recourse</h3>
    <p>If you disagree with the school district's decisions regarding your child's IEP, placement, or disciplinary actions, you have the right to due process under IDEA (20 U.S.C. § 1415) and NYS Education Law. Due process is a formal legal process that allows you to resolve disputes with the school district.</p>

    <p><b>When to Consider Due Process:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li>The school district refuses to conduct an FBA or implement a BIP.</li>
        <li>You disagree with the MDR determination.</li>
        <li>The school district fails to implement the IEP.</li>
        <li>You disagree with the proposed IEP or placement.</li>
        <li>The school district suspends your child for an extended period without proper due process.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Due Process Options:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Mediation:</b> A voluntary process where a neutral third party helps you and the school district reach a mutually agreeable resolution.</li>
        <li><b>Impartial Hearing:</b> A formal legal proceeding before an Impartial Hearing Officer (IHO) who will hear evidence and make a decision about the dispute. You have the right to be represented by an attorney. The IHO's decision is binding, but can be appealed to State Review Office (SRO).</li>
    </ul>

     <h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h3>

    <dl>
        <dt><b>Q: What if my child's behavior is dangerous?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: If a student's behavior poses an immediate threat to themselves or others, school personnel can take emergency measures, including temporary removal from the classroom. However, the school must still follow due process procedures and conduct an MDR if the removal exceeds 10 days or constitutes a pattern of removals. Positive Behavioral Interventions and Supports (PBIS) should be prioritized, even in crisis situations.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Can my child be expelled from District 75?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: While expulsion is rare in District 75, students with disabilities can be subject to disciplinary measures, including suspension. However, the school must adhere to strict due process procedures and ensure that the student continues to receive FAPE. Expulsion is generally not an appropriate disciplinary measure for students whose behavior is a manifestation of their disability.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What is the role of the Committee on Special Education (CSE) in disciplinary matters?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: The CSE plays a crucial role in ensuring that students with disabilities receive appropriate educational services and are protected from discriminatory disciplinary practices. The CSE is responsible for developing and reviewing the student's IEP, conducting FBAs, and implementing BIPs. The CSE also participates in MDRs and ensures that the student's rights are protected throughout the disciplinary process.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Where can I find more information about special education rights in New York City?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: You can find more information about special education rights in New York City from the following resources:
            <ul>
                <li>New York State Education Department (NYSED): <a href="http://www.nysed.gov">www.nysed.gov</a></li>
                <li>Advocates for Children of New York (AFC): <a href="https://www.advocatesforchildren.org/">www.advocatesforchildren.org</a></li>
                <li>INCLUDEnyc: <a href="https://www.includenyc.org/">www.includenyc.org</a></li>
            </ul>
        </dd>
    </dl>

    <p>Remember, you are a vital member of your child's IEP team and have the right to advocate for their needs. Don't hesitate to seek assistance from advocacy organizations and legal professionals specializing in special education law. Your active participation and knowledge of your child's rights are essential to ensuring they receive a fair and appropriate education in District 75.</p>
</body>
